+
+## Opt-in verified HTTPS (dependent proposal)
+
+An event `request` or `download` can declare `"tls": {"ca_file": "certs/root.pem"}`.
+The CA path is relative to that plugin's directory, at most 128 bytes;
+absolute paths, any `..` sequence, duplicate separators and backslashes are
+rejected. The PEM file is capped at 8KB and must contain a certificate envelope.
+The TLS implementation parses the certificate and verifies the peer.
+
+A present `tls` field opts in even when its value is null, empty or of the wrong
+type. An invalid opt-in fails the request and retains the event; it never falls
+back to insecure HTTP. Verified requests require HTTPS with a nonempty
+authority, a fresh transport and an SNTP callback observed after the current
+drain starts its synchronization attempt. A saved clock floor or an earlier
+SNTP `COMPLETED` status does not qualify. A direct verified HTTP call requires
+the internal callback proof, which expires within 30 seconds under both the
+monotonic and wall clocks. A new failed drain attempt invalidates prior proof.
+SNTP here does not authenticate UTC cryptographically.
+
+Userinfo and query/fragment text within the authority are rejected. Write
+`https://example.test/?token=...`, with a slash before any query; raw control
+characters, NUL and backslashes are rejected. The SDK can log the hostname, so
+never place credentials in a hostname. CP verified failure diagnostics omit the
+substituted URL, headers and body; this is not a guarantee about all SDK debug logs.
+
+If a verified event receives 401/403, password refresh runs only when
+`auth.request` also declares `tls.ca_file`. Both the mint and the retry use their
+own verified transport. Put bearer tokens in headers. Verified failure logs omit
+substituted URLs, headers and bodies. Requests without `tls` retain their existing
+behavior. The catalog's shared legacy session does not support this opt-in and
+rejects manifests that declare `tls` on browse, download or auth requests/polls.
+
+This proposal depends on an SDK implementation that checks CA-load failures and
+matches the peer certificate to the requested hostname. The current SDK pin does
+not provide that contract. The paired SDK proposal exposes
+`SecureHttpClient::peerVerificationAvailable()`: true for the wolfSSL backend
+with both checks, false for an unsupported backend. This is a backend capability,
+not proof that a particular connection was verified. CP still supplies the CA,
+requires a fresh clock proof and rejects caller-owned sessions for each request.
+
+If the SDK has no capability method, CP builds but rejects verified requests
+before synchronizing the clock or opening a socket. It does not enable verification
+from a local build flag. The method and the SDK reference are proposals pending
+upstream acceptance; this patch does not advance the SDK gitlink.
+
+## Timed sleep panels (dependent proposal)
+
+`sleep.timer` is a separate timer route, not an outbox event. It is never
+queued or replayed by an ordinary event drain. The proposed SDK
+capability above is required; older or unsupported SDKs cannot arm this route.
+This proposal is not yet available in an official CrossPoint release.
+
+Select **Sleep refresh source → SD plugin** and enable the Plugin System before
+arming this prototype path. The source setting defaults to Off. A previous
+local timed-wallpaper setting migrates to Local wallpapers, never to the
+network consumer. Local wallpapers rotate BMP files from `/.sleep` using their
+own interval and the Custom renderer, without Wi-Fi or the network battery
+threshold. They do not use `/sleep.bmp` or the legacy `/sleep` directory.
+A missing prerequisite for the selected source disables that attempt rather
+than selecting the other source. See [timed refresh](timed-refresh.md).
+
+A single subscribed plugin may provide this handler in `device.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "token": {"file": "token.json"},
+  "events": {
+    "sleep.timer": {
+      "interval_seconds": 900,
+      "download": {
+        "url": "https://example.test/frame",
+        "headers": {"Authorization": "Bearer {token}"},
+        "dest": "/sleep.bmp",
+        "format": "bmp",
+        "width": 480,
+        "height": 800,
+        "tls": {"ca_file": "certs/root.pem"}
+      }
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Dimensions must match the active board's default portrait axes; 480 by 800
+is the X4 Pro example, not a universal size. The interval must be an integer
+from 60 to 86400 seconds. The request must be HTTPS GET with an empty body,
+an explicit trust anchor, a nonempty saved token and a saved Wi-Fi network.
+Multiple timer owners, malformed configuration or missing prerequisites
+prevent arming. These interval bounds are prototype policy, not a measured
+battery-life or latency promise. Each successful clock sync may update the
+persisted time floor. At a 60-second cadence this can reach about 1,440 NVS
+updates per day; updates are not sector erases. Device qualification must
+measure energy, heap and NVS usage at the chosen cadence before release.
+
+The network caller uses the ESP deep-sleep timer on Sticky and X4 Pro. PaperMono uses
+an experimental RX8130 countdown and the PMIC GPIO0 wake input; it still needs
+physical wake, power and brownout qualification. At less than 20 percent
+battery, the reader returns to button-only sleep. A cycle has a separate
+10-second Wi-Fi join window, then one cooperative 15-second budget for fresh time, fetch and
+authentication, with at most four HTTP attempts. Blocking DNS/TCP, storage
+and panel work can exceed these windows. Do not describe them as hard limits.
+
+A successful HTTP 200 is staged in `/sleep.bmp.part`. Header and length checks
+are followed by a full row decode into the existing framebuffer. Only then
+does the existing checked replacement promote the file and request one
+HALF_REFRESH. After a successful replacement, HALF_REFRESH completes before a captured
+Power press is handled. Failed fetch, validation or decode preserves the previous asset
+and requests no transfer. HTTP 204/304 is a failed replacement in this slice;
+there is no conditional-refresh contract. The 1 MiB download limit also applies:
+a 480 by 800 uncompressed 24-bit BMP is too large; use a compatible smaller
+palette such as 1-bit BW. The timer path projects the raster into BW without
+scaling or applying saved Custom orientation/grayscale preferences.
+
+On Sticky and X4 Pro, a GPIO edge latch captures a Power press across blocking
+timer work. It uses the existing board pin/polarity through the HAL and is
+detached before normal
+input routing. If capture cannot be established, the autonomous work is
+skipped and the route returns to button-only sleep without initializing SD
+or the panel. This is not an unconditional guarantee of short-press capture.
+The shared GPIO ISR service keeps its existing interrupt flags; a new
+service uses normal interrupts, without a cache-off delivery guarantee. A
+captured press enters the existing click/hold and recovery path;
+a rejected short tap returns to standby without consuming a later held Power
+press during teardown. If deep-sleep entry returns after Power was captured, a one-shot RTC marker
+carries the press through a software restart into the manual path with
+peripherals initialized. Every boot clears the marker; only a software reset
+honors it. The final check-to-sleep interval still needs device qualification.
+This does not make the blocking I/O itself cancellable.
+
+PaperMono requires a running RTC with its voltage-loss flag cleared by setting
+valid time. Connect through the existing Wi-Fi setup or use Clock Sync first;
+the timer path does not invent a calendar value or clear that flag to enable
+itself. Intervals above 65,535 seconds round up to whole minutes. The first
+countdown period can be up to one second short, so this is not a precise
+wall-clock scheduler.
+
+The PaperMono route accepts a cold power-on only when the saved PMIC source
+is exclusively external GPIO wake, GPIO0 is the only enabled wake input, and
+the RTC reports an enabled timer interrupt with no clock-loss flag. It then
+cancels the countdown and wake input before starting the shared timer caller.
+Button, USB, software and brownout resets do not qualify. PMIC GPIO0 and the
+RTC interrupt output must not be shared with another wake owner.
+
+Power capture on PaperMono reads the PMIC's latched button event instead of
+installing an ESP GPIO interrupt. A failed read aborts autonomous work. The
+route checks again after the PMIC's command-settle delay and before shutdown.
+If shutdown returns, it cancels the timer and restarts normal reader startup;
+it never falls through to ESP-only sleep, which has no Power wake on this board.
+The remaining check-to-shutdown race needs a physical button test.
+
+The RTC route holds the PMIC LDO during shutdown, following M5Stack's RTC wake
+example. Its standby current has not been measured; figures from variants
+that release that rail do not apply. This integration uses the SDK
+RTC and PMIC APIs merged in #138; the CP timer path is not a released PaperMono capability.
+
+The timer branch does not load or save book position, reading history or reader
+outboxes. It restores the global plugin permission before discovery without
+loading or saving general reader settings. An offline or revoked grant may leave old pixels visible;
+include the content's timestamp in the image. Download or transfer completion
+is not proof that a person saw the screen. Device pixels, energy, runtime heap
+and the complete supported-model matrix require separate hardware acceptance.
+
+## Global plugin permission during timer boot
+
+The timer preflight restores only `pluginsEnabled` before discovering handlers.
+It does not run general settings loading, migration or resave. Ordinary sleep
+continues to use the in-memory setting. The same discovery path still applies the
+global switch, plugin root priority and installation quarantine.
+
+A missing, unreadable, invalid or oversized settings file leaves the timer off.
+The early read is capped at 16 KiB and validates the complete JSON object before
+assigning only the permission field. Parse or allocation failure leaves permission
+off. An absent or null field in a valid settings object follows the existing
+legacy-plugin detection rule; normal boot remains responsible for any resave.
+An explicitly disabled plugin system never gains permission from files on SD.
+This is a dependent integration with the proposed Plugin Hub/discovery contract,
+not a new account, remote-settings or installation mechanism.
